.TH CHOPIN 1 "2026-08" "chopin 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
chopin \- copy files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chopin
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fB\-T\fR] \fISOURCE\fR \fIDEST\fR
.br
.B chopin
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fISOURCE\fR... \fIDIRECTORY\fR
.br
.B chopin
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fB\-t\fR \fIDIRECTORY\fR \fISOURCE\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Copy
.I SOURCE
to
.IR DEST ,
or multiple
.I SOURCE
arguments to
.IR DIRECTORY .
.PP
.B chopin
is a from-scratch C11 reimplementation of GNU
.BR cp (1),
behavior-faithful to coreutils 9.11: the same options, the same
result trees, the same diagnostics byte-for-byte, and the same exit
status, except for the registered corrections in
.B DEVIATIONS
below.
.B cpn
is the same program under a shorter name; the name affects only the
program prefix in diagnostics.
.PP
Option semantics are those of GNU cp 9.11; see
.BR cp (1).
The full 9.11 option surface is accepted, including
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-\-attributes\-only ,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-\-backup ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-\-debug ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-\-preserve ,
.BR \-\-no\-preserve ,
.BR \-\-parents ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-\-reflink ,
.BR \-\-remove\-destination ,
.BR \-\-sparse ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-strip\-trailing\-slashes ,
.BR \-\-suffix ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-\-update ,
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-Z ,
and
.BR \-\-context .
.SH PERFORMANCE
chopin copies with a device-aware worker pool and the platform's
fastest transfer path: reflink cloning
.RB ( FICLONE
on Linux,
.BR fclonefileat (2)
on macOS),
.BR copy_file_range (2)
offload, and
.B SEEK_HOLE
sparse detection, with a scalar read/write loop as the always-present
fallback.
.PP
Ordering is unaffected by parallelism: all output is emitted on the
traversal spine, so a parallel run is byte-identical to a serial one.
Rotational and network device pairs are detected and copied serially.
Directories are traversed in sorted name order, which is
deterministic across runs and filesystems (GNU traverses in inode
order).
.PP
Measured results, the methodology, and the one workload where chopin
is slower than GNU cp are recorded in the project README and in
.IR bench/ledger.md .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The usual locale variables apply
.RB ( LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_MESSAGES ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ),
along with
.BR POSIXLY_CORRECT ,
.BR SIMPLE_BACKUP_SUFFIX ,
.BR VERSION_CONTROL ,
and
.B TMPDIR
as in GNU cp.
.PP
The following variables are chopin extensions. They exist for
debugging, testing, and benchmarking; none of them change
user-visible output.
.TP
.B CHOPIN_PARALLEL_WORKERS
Worker count for the copy pool.
.B 0
disables the pool entirely (fully serial). Unset selects the logical
core count, capped at 16 and clamped so worker file descriptors stay
within
.BR RLIMIT_NOFILE .
.TP
.B CHOPIN_PARALLEL_MIN
Aggregate floor before the pool activates, as a file count and a byte
count.
.B 1
dispatches every eligible payload; the test suites pin this.
.TP
.B CHOPIN_PARALLEL_CHUNKS
Set to
.B 1
to split large single files into disjoint-offset chunks across
workers. Off by default: no measured win on the benchmarked
filesystems.
.TP
.B CHOPIN_CHUNK_THRESHOLD
Minimum file size in bytes for chunked dispatch (default 64 MiB).
.TP
.B CHOPIN_FORCE_SCALAR
Set to
.B 1
to disable
.BR copy_file_range (2)
offload and force the scalar read/write loop.
.TP
.B CHOPIN_DEBUG_PLAN
Print the engine and parallelism plan to standard error.
.TP
.B CHOPIN_DEBUG_STATS
Print reflink probe and probe-cache counters at exit.
.TP
.B CHOPIN_DEBUG_VERIFY
Re-read every destination after copying and compare it against the
source; abort on any mismatch. This is the correctness oracle for the
fast paths and is enabled throughout the test suites.
.TP
.B CHOPIN_DEBUG_WALK
Traverse sources and run the full decision ladder without creating,
writing, or modifying anything. Used to measure traversal cost.
.PP
GNU cp's own
.B \-\-debug
output is reproduced byte-for-byte.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All requested copies succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred. As in GNU cp, there are only these two statuses.
.SH DEVIATIONS
chopin matches GNU cp 9.11 exactly, including its documented quirks,
except for the entries below. Entries marked
.B FIX
are defects in GNU cp that chopin corrects rather than reproduces;
each has an upstream report and a pinned regression test. Entries
marked
.B QUIRK-KEPT
were investigated and deliberately matched anyway. Entries marked
.B BUILD-PARITY
are not deviations: they record the feature set of the GNU build that
chopin is compared against.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED DEVIATIONS - edit doc/deviations.md, run make man
.TP
.B DEV-001 (FIX)
double-space backup diagnostic.
.sp
GNU: `backing up %s might destroy source;  %s not copied` with two
spaces after the semicolon (copy.c:1934-1935) - a malformed message,
quirk register 2. chopin prints a single space.
Upstream: draft ready; filing recorded here when done.
Pinned by: dev-tier case (sprint 03).
.TP
.B DEV-002 (FIX)
unquoted symlink-chown failure operand.
.sp
GNU: `failed to preserve ownership for %s` prints the destination
UNQUOTED in the symlink-ownership failure path (copy.c:2579-2580),
inconsistent with every neighboring diagnostic (quirk register 3).
chopin quotes it.
Upstream: draft ready.
Pinned by: code site (src/copy.c symlink branch). The failure needs
lchownat to fail with a non-benign errno, which requires privileges
(chown_failure_ok silences the unprivileged EPERM) - unreachable in
the root-refusing harness; the fuzz tier cannot draw it either.
.TP
.B DEV-003 (QUIRK-KEPT)
--update=older identical-symlink success.
.sp
GNU treats a failed symlink creation as success when the existing
dest is an identical symlink under --update=older (copy.c:2541-2557);
GNU's own comment says the behavior "isn't documented, and seems
wrong". Kept for v0.1: scripts may depend on it; revisit with
upstream discussion. Matched exactly (sprint 05).
.TP
.B DEV-004 (FIX)
same_nameat abrupt exit.
.sp
GNU: same_nameat calls error(1,...) mid-comparison when a parent
stat fails (lib/same.c:101,139), aborting the whole run instead of
failing the one file (quirk register 21). chopin reports
`cannot stat %s` and fails only that file.
Upstream: draft ready.
Pinned by: unit (samefile_driver dev004-parent-stat) - the failing
parent stat needs a mid-comparison race to reach through the CLI, so
the pin exercises the function surface directly.
.TP
.B DEV-005 (FIX)
sparse_copy hole-punch success-on-failure.
.sp
GNU: sparse_copy returns success (0) when create_hole fails mid-file
(copy-file-data.c:237-238) - cp prints an error yet exits 0 with a
TRUNCATED destination; in lseek_copy the 0 is misread as "input file
shrank" (433-437). Data-loss class (quirk register 34). chopin
propagates the failure: nonzero exit, copy_reg failure envelope.
Upstream: draft ready.
Pinned by: code site (create_hole in src/copydata.c returns -1 and
every caller propagates). A real mid-file punch failure needs an
environment fault the sandbox cannot stage deterministically (GNU's
own make_holes gate excludes non-regular dests); sprint 08's
bounded-ENOSPC fuzz lane exercises the path.
.TP
.B DEV-006 (BUILD-PARITY)
build-configuration parity notes.
.sp
Not deviations - chopin matches GNU cp built with the pinned oracle
feature set (scripts/build-gnu-cp.sh): no SELinux (-Z warn/ignore
paths), no POSIX-ACL support (mode bits only; ACL xattrs travel via
the xattr pass), xattr support ON with /etc/xattr.conf exclusions
not read (golden fixtures use user.* names only; sprint 04).
.\" END GENERATED DEVIATIONS
